<% content_for :own_heading, true %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl">
  <%= render "admin/component_demo/environment_banner" %>

  <%= admin_breadcrumbs(@breadcrumbs) %>

  <% description = "Every question in design-todo.md that is still open, with what the app does today " \
                   "beside what I would do. Built to decide, deleted once decided." %>
  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: "Open questions", description: description } do %><% end %>

  <div class="space-y-10">
    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1 %>
    <section data-testid="question-form-card">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">1. A form card's own heading</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Ten admin form partials hand-roll <code>&lt;div class="tw-card"&gt;&lt;div class="p-5"&gt;</code>
        rather than using <code>components/ui/_card</code>, and each opens with an
        <code>h3</code> naming the record type. The surface is declared eleven times, which is the
        mechanism that produced four different padding values before.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">Today</p>
          <div class="tw-card">
            <div class="p-5">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-slate-900 mb-6">Teacher details</h3>
              <p class="tw-label-primary">Email address</p>
              <div class="tw-input-primary flex items-center text-slate-500">terry@example.com</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-600">
            Under a page header reading "Edit teacher". The card says the same thing again, in a
            different size, 24px lower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">Recommended</p>
          <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
            <p class="tw-label-primary">Email address</p>
            <div class="tw-input-primary flex items-center text-slate-500">terry@example.com</div>
          <% end %>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-600">
            <code>components/ui/_card</code>, no heading. Polaris drops a card's header when the page
            has one card; the page title already named the thing. A heading returns the moment a form
            has two cards - <code>admin/students</code> does - because then it separates them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2 %>
    <section data-testid="question-card-padding">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">2. Is a form card <code>p-5</code> or <code>p-6</code>?</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        design.md says <code>p-5</code> and the app now says <code>p-5</code> everywhere, so this is
        settled as a consistency question. It is open as a value question: the admin half independently
        chose 24px in ten places. shadcn's Card is 24px, Polaris is 16/20px.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">p-5 - today, and recommended</p>
          <div class="tw-card p-5" data-testid="pad-5">
            <p class="tw-label-primary">Classroom name</p>
            <div class="tw-input-primary flex items-center text-slate-500">5B</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">p-6</p>
          <div class="tw-card p-6" data-testid="pad-6">
            <p class="tw-label-primary">Classroom name</p>
            <div class="tw-input-primary flex items-center text-slate-500">5B</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Keep <code>p-5</code>. The difference is 4px a side and the document has decided; the cost of
        changing it is another app-wide sweep and a paragraph in design.md explaining why a form card is
        special. If it ever changes, it changes because a form is measurably cramped at 375px - and it is
        not: <code>p-5</code> leaves a field 286px wide there, <code>p-6</code> 278px.
      </p>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3 %>
    <section data-testid="question-classroom-stats">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">3. Four figures computed and never rendered</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        <code>ClassroomsController#show</code> sets <code>@classroom_stats</code> for a teacher or admin -
        four figures, four queries - and no template reads it. Separately, "how is 5B invested?" has no
        answer anywhere. <strong>These are one question.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-4">
        <%= render "components/ui/stat", label: "Students", value: @stats[:total_students].to_s %>
        <%= render "components/ui/stat",
                   label: "Have traded",
                   value: @stats[:active_students].to_s,
                   hint: "Placed at least one order" %>
        <%= render "components/ui/stat",
                   label: "Class portfolio",
                   value: number_to_currency((@stats[:total_portfolio_value] || 0) / 100.0) %>
        <%= render "components/ui/stat",
                   label: "Orders this week",
                   value: @stats[:recent_orders_count].to_s %>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Rendered above from the real facade, for <%= @classroom&.name || "no classroom" %>. Recommended:
        <strong>render them, at the foot of <code>classrooms#show</code></strong>, not the top. Measured on
        that page at 1366x768 - viewport 625px, Students at 168px, Grade books at 420px, page ends at
        637px - a band above the roster once put the first student at 567px of 625px. Below the grade
        books it is the summary you scroll to after the work, which is when the question is asked.
        Deleting the four queries is the alternative and it is worse: the figures are the answer to a
        question a teacher has.
      </p>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4 %>
    <section data-testid="question-perfect-attendance">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">4. Perfect attendance, ticked by hand</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        A teacher types the day count, then answers a second control saying the student attended every
        day. In the seeds one entry carries the flag with <code>attendance_days</code> nil and is paid the
        <%= number_to_currency(GradeEntry::EARNINGS_FOR_PERFECT_ATTENDANCE / 100.0) %> bonus; another
        treats 3 days as perfect. The two fields can disagree, and the money follows the flag.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">Today</p>
          <div class="tw-card p-5">
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">Days attended <span class="font-semibold">3</span></p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">Perfect attendance
              <span class="font-semibold">Yes</span></p>
            <%= render "components/ui/callout",
                       tone: :warning,
                       title: "Two answers, one fact",
                       body: "Nothing reconciles them, and the bonus follows the flag." %>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold text-slate-500">Recommended</p>
          <div class="tw-card p-5">
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">Days attended <span class="font-semibold">3</span></p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700">
              Perfect attendance
              <span class="font-semibold">No</span>
              <span class="text-slate-600">- derived from 3 of 45 school days</span>
            </p>
            <%= render "components/ui/callout",
                       tone: :info,
                       title: "One field, one fact",
                       body: "The flag becomes a derived reading of days against the quarter's school days." %>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-600">
            Blocked on one thing: <code>quarters.school_days</code> does not exist, so there is no
            denominator. That column is the whole change - after it, the flag is a method.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5 %>
    <section data-testid="question-archived-stocks">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">5. Should the archived stocks table exist?</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        It has a date, a 12-month window and an exemption for anything you hold, which makes it
        defensible. The question was never asked: <em>who opens it, and to do what?</em>
      </p>
      <div class="tw-card p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
          Recommended: <strong>keep the "Archived stocks you hold" table, drop the disclosure below it.</strong>
          The first answers a real need - a student must be able to sell something that stopped trading. The
          second is a list of companies nobody can buy, behind a
          <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, retained for a year. A teacher who wants the full catalogue has
          <code>/admin/stocks</code>; a student has no use for it at all. Deleting it removes a table, a
          scope, a retention constant and a test - and nothing on any page changes for anyone who was not
          already scrolling past a closed disclosure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6 %>
    <section data-testid="question-user-restore">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">6. An archived plain user has no way back</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        <code>admin/users</code> archives and nothing un-archives. <code>admin/students#restore</code> and
        the teacher reactivation cover the two subclasses; a user who is neither has no route back, and
        the row simply shows no action.
      </p>
      <div class="tw-card p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
          Recommended: <strong>add <code>restore</code> to <code>admin/users</code></strong>. It is the same
          shape as the students one, it covers every type at once - including the two that already have
          their own - and it turns "no action on this row" into an action. The alternative, writing down
          that a plain user is archived forever, is a policy nobody chose: the discard is reversible in the
          data and irreversible only in the interface.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7 %>
    <section data-testid="question-environment-ribbon">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">7. A whole-app environment ribbon</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        The component demo names its environment because the page is not part of the product. A ribbon
        answers a different question - <em>which deployment am I looking at</em> - and staging exists here
        with no sign of it.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 bg-amber-100 px-4 py-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-amber-900">
          <%= lucide_icon "triangle-alert", class: "size-4 shrink-0" %>
          Staging - this data is not real
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white px-4 py-3">
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">The page, unchanged beneath it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        Recommended: <strong>build it for staging only, and not for development.</strong> A strip above the
        header bar, not a callout in the content - it describes the whole application, not the page, which
        is where GitLab and Shopify put theirs. No dismiss, by this app's own rule: the environment is still
        true in a minute. In development the URL already says localhost, and a permanent stripe on every
        page of every working day is noise that teaches you to stop seeing it - which is exactly what would
        make it useless on staging.
      </p>
    </section>

    <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8 %>
    <section data-testid="question-buy-sell-alignment">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">8. Buy and Sell sit below their row's first line</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 mb-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
        The trading floor's controls are 40px buttons in a row whose primary cell stacks a 40px logo over
        two lines, so the row is 65px and the buttons' centre is 10.5px below the ticker. The
        <code>pt-1.5</code> used for 32px row actions is the wrong number here.
      </p>
      <div class="tw-card p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
          Recommended: <strong>align the cell, not the button</strong> - <code>align-top</code> on the
          actions cell and let the 40px control sit at the top of the row, which is what design.md already
          says for every other trailing cell. The alternative, a per-cell padding tuned to this row's
          height, breaks the moment a company name wraps to three lines. Worth measuring the row again
          first: the logo is what makes the row 65px, and it is decoration.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
